####################################
####################################
Description:
####################################
####################################

Files requiered to reproduce the remote sensing reflectance (RRS) assimilation experiments as a function of constant inflation, as well as with an online
noise estimation method, for both, an indirect assimilation as well as a direct one.
The configuration files and forcings were download from zenodo: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10067424, A GOTM-FABM-BFM setup to run 1D simulations
at the Boussole site used in Álvarez et al. (2023): Chromophoric dissolved organic matter dynamics revealed through the optimization of a
spectrally and vertically resolved model in the NW Mediterranean Sea.
Since the experiments were performed in a cluster, due to limiting space, we are sharing the results of only the variables used for comparizon
with in-situ observations. 

The remote sensing reflectance assimilation experiments are done using the Ensemble and Assimilation Tool (EAT, https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-17-5619-2024),
a python3 tool which uses the Parallel Data Assimilation Framework (PDAF, https://pdaf.awi.de/trac/wiki), a Fortran based softward environment to perform
data assimilation. EAT works by runing an ensemble of the physico-biogeochemical model in parallel, everytime there is an observation available, stops the
simulation, performs an analysis step, and then continues the simulation.

The interaction between the biogeochemical model, in our case the Biogeochemical Flux Model (BFM) and the physical component, with the General Ocean
Turbulence Model (GOTM), is handled by the Framework for Aquatic Biogeochemical Models (FABM). In addition, Alvarez, et al 2023, included the bio-optical
component to the model, making possible the direct assimilation of rrs.


####################################
####################################
Requirements for reproducing the results
####################################
####################################
	-CMAKE > 3.5
	-EAT: The EAT tool can be installed with anaconda (see https://github.com/BoldingBruggeman/eat/wiki/, last visit August 7, 2026).
	It installs GOTM and FABM with their default configurations.
	It can also be download from github in https://github.com/BoldingBruggeman/eat or from zenodo, DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.10306435.

	-BFM: In addition to the EAT tool, FABM and GOTM, it will be necesary to install the BFM model configure to work with FABM, the original
	version is available in github (https://github.com/inogs/bfmforfabm.git, last visit August 7, 2026) or zenodo (10.5281/zenodo.10067423).
	We included the version of the OASIM model for fabm that we used for the assimilation, where we included the variables NAP and CDOM as part of the
	outputs of the model (./reference/light_spectral_OASIM.F90).

	-Both BFM and EAT are fortran based, so it is needed a fortran compiler, while eat also uses python version >= 3.8. To install EAT with the
	anaconda method, it is needed to have installed Anaconda or Miniconda, and to have mpi4py installed for the parallel computations.

	-The experiments were originally performed in a cluster with Slurm workload manager, but it is not necesary to run the comands, except those
	in files called jobParallelSlurm.

####################################
####################################
Installing the necesary software to reproduce the results
####################################
####################################

	To reproduce the results is enough to install EAT, the BFM for fabm, and the OASIM model for fabm, next i add the command lines to do so
	with github:
	
	-Downloading fabm-spectral, model of the incomming irradiance, branch split. 
	
		$git clone --recursive https://github.com/pmlmodelling/fabm-spectral.git ./extern/spectral
		$cd extern/spectral
		$git checkout split
		$cd ../../
		     
	-Downloading bfm for fabm, branch neccton
	
		$git clone --recursive https://github.com/inogs/bfmforfabm.git ./extern/ogs
		$cd extern/ogs
		$git checkout neccton
		$cd ../../

        -Modify the light_spectral_OASIM.F90 file for the one we used (includes the definitions for NAP and CDOM)
	        $mv extern/ogs/light_spectral_OASIM.F90 extern/ogs/light_spectral_OASIM.F90_original
		$cp reference/light_spectral_OASIM.F90 extern/ogs/

	-Downloading EAT, and installing it

		$git clone --recursive https://github.com/BoldingBruggeman/eat.git ./extern/eat
		$cd extern/eat
		$conda env create -f environment.yml
		$conda activate eat
		$source ./install -DFABM_INSTITUTES="ogs;spectral" -DFABM_OGS_BASE=../ogs -DFABM_SPECTRAL_BASE=../spectral

        -Installing the pyhton3 module ruamel for managing yaml files. 

		$conda install ruamel.yaml



	-Creating experiments:

		$python3 create_forgeting_factors_experiments.py -hp <path/to/rrs_assimilation> -dp <path/to/rrs_assimilation> -N <Number_of_ensemble_members>

	where <path/to/rrs_assimilation> is the path to the folder where u have the folders gotms, fabms, reference, forcing and observations, and 
	<Number_of_ensemble_members> is the number of ensemble members per experiment, which has to be (number of cores available - 1) if the simulation
	 of each ensemble member is performe with one core. 


####################################
####################################
File description
####################################
####################################

-reference:

	The folder reference contain the baseline files needed to simulate the biogeochemistry properties at the Bouée pour l’acquisition d’une Série
	 Optique à Long terme (BUOSSOLE) site, located in the Ligurian Sea, subbasin of the Western Mediterranean Sea, approximately at the
	 coordinates 7 degrees 54 minuts E, 43 degrees 22 minuts N, setup download from  Alvarez, 2023 (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.10067424).
	 The original fabm is called fabm_original.yaml, the one used with some modifications in the parameter values is fabm.yam.
	 Toguether with the folder ../forcing, runing:

	    $eat-gotm

	inside this folder should work as long as it contains the files fabm.yaml, gotm.yaml, restart.nc, the forlder bcs and the folder ../forcings,
	and the installation of EAT, with BFM and the spectral component was correctly performed. 

	The output is a netcdf with all the variables called result.nc, and a file restart.nc, with the value of the state variables at the end of the
	 simulation.

	 -bcs
		 contains the Spectral absorption and total scattering coefficients for water, chlorophyll, cdom and particulate organic carbon needed
		 to run the bio optical component of the model implemented by  Alvarez et al, 2023 (Chromophoric dissolved organic matter dynamics
		 revealed through the optimization of an optical--biogeochemical model in the northwestern Mediterranean Sea).

-forcings:

	These folder has the forcings needed to run the BFM coupled with GOTM and the bio-optical components. It has the same forcings as the ones
	 used by Alvarez, 2023, plus the eastward gradient of preassure (ugos.csv) and the northward gradient of preassure (vgos.csv).

-observations:

	The ensemble and assimilation tool (EAT) runs the model, and simultaniusly check text files with observations. Every time an observation is
	 encounter, EAT stops the simulation, executes an assimilation step with the new observation changing the state of the system, and initialize
	 the simulation with the new states. The observations needed for the assimilation are in this folder. 
	For my work, I used three types of observations:

	   *log transform of the buoy sea surface chlorophyll: chla_std.txt.
	   *log transform of the remote sensing reflectance: rrs_425.txt, rrs_450.txt, rrs_500.txt, rrs_525.txt, rrs_550.txt.
	   *log transform of the remote sensing sea surface chlorophyll, nap and cdom: chla_inverted.txt, nap_inverted.txt, cdom_inverted.txt.

-fabms:
	The goal of the work was to analyse the performance of an ensemble based data assimilation. For this end, we created an ensemble of simulations
	with different initial conditions and parameterizations. To ensure reproducibility, the parameterizations corresponding
	to the biogeochemical and bio-optical model used during our work are in the different fabm_#.yaml files. 

-gotms:
	The goal of the work was to analyse the performance of an ensemble based data assimilation. For this end, we created an ensemble of simulations
	with different initial conditions and parameterizations. To ensure reproducibility, the parameterizations corresponding
	to the physical component of the model used during our work are in the different gotm_#.yaml files.

-initializations:
	The goal of the work was to analyse the performance of an ensemble based data assimilation. For this end, we created an ensemble of simulations
	with different initial conditions and parameterizations. To ensure reproducibility, the initicializations corresponding
	to the physical component of the model used during our work are in the different restart_#.yaml files.

-plugins:
	Two main plugins were used during the assimilation steps, in addition to the main plugins already available in the EAT tool.
	
	    *transform_clean.py: Log transform the state variables, and force the output values to be between the ranges:
	    			     x > max(gamma_min,gamma_)
				     x < max(gamma_max,gamma+)
	     			 where gamma_ = 1E-9, gamma+ = 4, and gamma_min and gamma_max are the minimum and maximum value of the variable x during an
				 assimilation without assimilation. This procedure showed to help to avoid divergences, hopping it didn't has a strong effect
				 in the final results. We also add noise with zero mean, uniformly distributed between −0.1E−9 and 0.1E−9 to
				 variables with zero variance.

	    *online_noise.py: 	 This plug in adds noise and every time an observation is available, adjust the amplitude of the noise added
	    			 according to an online-noise estimation scheem.
				 
	We also included the plugin used to store the average statistics of an ensemble simulation without data assimilation.

-da_chla_assimilation, da_chla_buoy_assimilation, da_cha_nap_cdom_assimilation, da_rrs_assimilation:
	Folders where the experiments with constant inflation, and the experiments with online-noise estimation will be stored with the defoult settings by
	running

	$python3 create_forgeting_factors_experiments.py -hp <path/to/rrs_assimilation> -dp <path/to/rrs_assimilation> -N <Number_of_ensemble_members>

	where <path/to/rrs_assimilation> is the path to the folder where u have the folders gotms, fabms, reference, forcing and observations, and 
	<Number_of_ensemble_members> is the number of ensemble members per experiment, which has to be (number of cores available - 1) if the simulation
	 of each ensemble member is performe with one core.

-extern:
	Folder where the softward EAT, BFM (ogs) and the OASIM for FABM (Spectral) will be stored if the steps in
	"Installing the necesary software to reproduce the results" are followed. 

